The interviews are done. Here are the results of the human-validated gates.

[ONE LINE PER CANDIDATE PER FACTOR, e.g.

   ANGIE — client-facing: YES
   ANGIE — leadership: ? (not tested)
   ANGIE — craft: ? (asked, not shown)
   ANGIE — response to challenge: ? (not tested)
   LEON   — client-facing: NO
   LEON   — leadership: NO
   ... and so on]

Reissue the full report as the POST-INTERVIEW ISSUE. Change these things and nothing else.

PAGE 1 — SUMMARY
  The gate column becomes STATUS. Its values are:
    CLEAR every gate passed. Use this only when every line for that candidate is YES.
    OPEN   no gate failed, but at least one is still unresolved.
    ENDED one or more gates failed.
  The recommendation column becomes "where it stands after the interviews" — one line per candidate saying what the room decided and what it forces next.

PAGE 2 — THE COMPLETED GATE SHEET
  Replace the empty boxes with the marks I have given you, using exactly this vocabulary and no other:
    YES          gate cleared
    NO           gate failed — the candidacy ends on this line
    ?            tested but not settled, or asked but not demonstrated. Still open.
    NOT REACHED the candidate had already ended on an earlier line; never assessed
  Where I gave you a short note — "asked, not shown", "not tested" — print it under the mark.
  Add a header strip: interviewer, date, the format of the interviews, and the status.
  Add an OUTCOME row at the foot of the grid: CLEAR, OPEN or ENDED per candidate.
  Add a legend under the grid explaining the four marks.
  Keep the three rules beneath it unchanged.

  Two things you must not do:
  - Do not convert a ? into a YES. A question answered in conversation is not a gate closed.
  - Do not leave any cell blank. Every cell carries one of the four marks.

CANDIDATE PAGES
  Unchanged. The evidence did not move because somebody was interviewed. Do not re-score anyone on the strength of how the conversation went — the scores measure the paper, the gates measure the room, and they are deliberately kept apart.

WRAP-UP
  Rewrite it for where it now stands: who remains, what the room decided that the paper could not, which gates are still open on the leading candidate, and what the next session has to test. If a gate that the scoring had weighted lightly turned out to decide the outcome, say so and say what its weight should be next time.

THE VERDICT
  If a candidate has every gate marked YES, say plainly that the seat can be filled.
  If nobody does, say plainly that the seat is not filled. Do not soften it, and do not describe an OPEN candidate as though they had cleared.
